  "name": "24 HR URINARY COPPER",
  "code": "1008",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p>A 24-hour urinary copper test measures the amount of copper excreted in urine over a full day. It is mainly used to evaluate copper metabolism and to help diagnose and monitor conditions such as Wilson&rsquo;s disease.</p>\n<div>Elevated urinary copper:</div>\n<ul>\n<li>Seen in Wilson&rsquo;s disease (a genetic disorder causing copper accumulation), where values often exceed 100 &mu;g/24 hours, sometimes much higher.</li>\n<li>May also be elevated in obstructive biliary diseases (e.g., primary biliary cirrhosis), nephrotic syndrome, or due to chelation therapy and certain medication effects.</li>\n<li>Inflammation or steroid hormone therapy can increase urine copper excretion.</li>\n</ul>\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n<div>Low copper excretion:</div>\n<div>\n<ul>\n<li>May suggest copper deficiency, malnutrition, or conditions like Menkes disease (a genetic disorder causing poor copper absorption)</li>\n</ul>\n</div>",
